    {
      "id": 3,
      "slug": "how-ocs-works",
      "title": "How OCS Works",
      "description_html": "",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "type": "image",
          "variant": "full",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "LIGHT",
            "card_mode": "WHITE",
            "image_opacity": 0.5,
            "image_opacity_color": "#000000",
            "zoom_on_click": false,
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 0,
            "padding_bottom": 0,
            "custom_padding_top": 0,
            "custom_padding_bottom": 0,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO",
            "entrance_animation": true
          },
          "image_role": "decorative",
          "media": {
            "path": "resources/images/intro-1.png",
            "alt": "Intro_1.png",
            "source_asset": "Intro_1.png",
            "mime_type": "image/png",
            "bytes": 93469,
            "dimensions": {
              "originalWidth": 1680,
              "originalHeight": 222
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "image",
          "variant": "text_aside",
          "style": {
            "image_position": "right",
            "image_opacity": 0.5,
            "image_opacity_color": "#000000",
            "zoom_on_click": true,
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 3,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO"
          },
          "image_role": "content",
          "media": {
            "path": "resources/images/4.jpg",
            "alt": "4.jpg",
            "source_asset": "4.jpg",
            "mime_type": "image/jpeg",
            "bytes": 139705,
            "dimensions": {
              "originalWidth": 1680,
              "originalHeight": 945
            }
          },
          "body_html": "<p>The core operating principle of Optical Circuit Switching is straightforward. OCS establishes <strong>direct photonic connections between endpoints</strong> without any Optical-Electrical-Optical (OEO) conversion. Because it operates purely in the optical domain, OCS is inherently <strong>protocol-agnostic</strong> and <strong>data-rate-agnostic</strong>.</p><p>Traditional Electrical Packet Switching (EPS) requires packet buffers and per-packet forwarding logic, which inherently add queuing delays. In contrast, OCS redirects continuous light paths. This makes the switch completely congestion-free and jitter-free, allowing it to act as a passively latency-neutral component in the network fabric.</p><p></p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "text",
          "variant": "paragraph",
          "style": {
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 3,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO"
          },
          "body_html": "<p>Explore the diagram below to compare <strong>OCS</strong> and <strong>EPS</strong> behavior across five critical <strong>networking dimensions</strong>.</p><p>Click on each <strong>marker</strong> to reveal the architectural <strong>tradeoffs</strong>.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "type": "labeled_graphic",
          "variant": "labeledgraphic",
          "style": {
            "background_color": "#ffffff",
            "zoom_on_click": true,
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 3,
            "media_width": "1",
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO",
            "media_width_variant": "medium"
          },
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "Latency",
              "x": "18.678080240885418",
              "y": "66.16843248801257",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<ul><li><p>OCS provides jitter-free, near-zero latency because it lacks packet buffers. </p></li><li><p>EPS introduces queuing delays during store-and-forward processing.</p></li></ul>"
            },
            {
              "title": "Power Consumption",
              "x": "27.642618815104164",
              "y": "33.11507936507937",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<ul><li><p>OCS consumes significantly less power by operating purely in the optical domain. </p></li><li><p>EPS requires high power to fuel repeated OEO conversions and ASIC processing.</p></li></ul>"
            },
            {
              "title": "Packet Handling",
              "x": "50.89285714285714",
              "y": "73.65079365079366",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<ul><li><p>OCS cannot perform per-packet forwarding, QoS enforcement, or traffic inspection. </p></li><li><p>EPS is designed specifically to route and inspect individual packets.</p></li></ul>"
            },
            {
              "title": "Scalability",
              "x": "60.11904761904761",
              "y": "40.317460317460316",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<ul><li><p>OCS natively supports future network speed upgrades because it is data-rate-agnostic. </p></li><li><p>EPS switches must be physically replaced to support higher data rates.</p></li></ul>"
            },
            {
              "title": "Insertion Loss",
              "x": "85",
              "y": "71.32275132275132",
              "icon": "01",
              "description_html": "<ul><li><p>OCS hop counts are typically limited to one or two due to passive optical insertion loss. </p></li><li><p>EPS can scale across many hops because the signal is regenerated electronically at every stage.</p></li></ul>"
            }
          ],
          "media": {
            "path": "resources/images/ocs-vs-eps.jpg",
            "alt": "OCS VS EPS.png",
            "source_asset": "OCS VS EPS.jpg",
            "mime_type": "image/jpeg",
            "bytes": 107951,
            "dimensions": {
              "originalWidth": 1680,
              "originalHeight": 945
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "type": "text",
          "variant": "subheading",
          "style": {
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 3,
            "padding_bottom": 0,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO"
          },
          "heading_html": "<p>These physical tradeoffs translate into concrete benefits and constraints when deploying optical switching at scale.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "type": "image",
          "variant": "full",
          "style": {
            "background_type": "LIGHT",
            "card_mode": "WHITE",
            "image_opacity": 0.5,
            "image_opacity_color": "#000000",
            "zoom_on_click": false,
            "attached_to_next": false,
            "padding_top": 0,
            "padding_bottom": 0,
            "custom_padding_top": 0,
            "custom_padding_bottom": 0,
            "marker_color_contrast": "AUTO",
            "entrance_animation": true
          },
          "image_role": "decorative",
          "media": {
            "path": "resources/images/separador-2.png",
            "alt": "Separador 2.png",
            "source_asset": "Separador 2.png",
            "mime_type": "image/png",
