    {
      "id": 5,
      "slug": "knowledge-check",
      "title": "Knowledge Check",
      "description_html": "<p>Apply your understanding of optical physics and architectural trade-offs to the following scenarios.<br><br>Select \"Start Quiz\" to begin.</p>",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>Which property allows OCS to remain compatible with future network speed upgrades without hardware replacement?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>High insertion loss</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Packet buffer optimization</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p> Data-rate-agnostic operation</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Differential packet handling</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Because OCS simply redirects light, it operates independent of the underlying protocol and data rate.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. OCS is data-rate-agnostic, which allows it to pass faster signals without requiring a hardware upgrade.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>What is the primary reason OCS cannot perform QoS enforcement or per-flow telemetry?</p><p></p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It lacks optical amplification</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>It operates purely in the optical domain without packet processing logic</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p> It has too much insertion loss</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>The switching speed is too slow</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Because there is no electrical conversion, OCS cannot read, inspect, or prioritize individual packets.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p>Incorrect. OCS lacks packet inspection and QoS capabilities precisely because it operates purely in the optical domain.</p>"
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "knowledge_check",
          "question_type": "multiple_choice",
          "question_html": "<p>A data center engineer wants to reduce spine-layer power consumption dramatically. Which technology approach does OCS enable?</p>",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text_html": "<p> Increasing OEO conversions</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p> Replacing EPS spine switches with buffer less optical paths</p>",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p> Adding deeper packet buffers</p>",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text_html": "<p>Implementing differential packet handling</p>",
              "correct": false
            }
          ],
          "feedback_correct_html": "<p>Correct. Replacing the EPS spine layer with OCS eliminates massive amounts of electrical processing, drastically reducing power consumption.</p>",
          "feedback_incorrect_html": "<p> Incorrect. OCS enables power reduction by replacing EPS spine switches with bufferless, purely optical paths.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
